     "standing": "NOT SEATED — declared absent",
     "why": "The foundational Sethian text, surviving in four manuscripts, and the closest thing Gnosticism has to a canonical scripture. No openly licensed machine-readable Coptic edition exists. Searched 2026-08-21: Coptic SCRIPTORIUM does not hold it; authenticated GitHub search returns one edition whose Coptic derives from scanned PDFs of Meyer 2007 and Linssen, relicensed CC BY-SA by a party that does not hold those rights; Schmidt 1905 is public domain but is the Pistis Sophia volume, and archive.org OCR strips Coptic script entirely; Till 1955, the Berlin Codex edition, remains in copyright.",
     "ruling": "Not seated rather than seated defectively. A corpus whose licence is asserted by a party who cannot grant it would place a rights defect inside the sovereign record — the failure this archive exists to document. Absent-and-declared over present-and-defective.",
     "opening": "One text, four witnesses. A synoptic open edition of the Apocryphon of John is the narrowest and highest-value gap in the openly licensed Coptic record.",
     "searched": "2026-08-21"
    },
    {
     "work": "Nag Hammadi Codices I and III–XIII",
     "standing": "NOT SEATED — declared absent",
     "why": "Fifty-one of the fifty-two tractates. Print editions only (Claremont, Brill); no open machine-readable Coptic.",
     "searched": "2026-08-21"
    },
    {
     "work": "The Qumran sectarian corpus (Dead Sea Scrolls)",
     "standing": "NOT SEATED — declared absent",
     "why": "Qumran-Digital publishes Open Access with a permissive robots.txt but is JS-rendered with no discoverable text endpoint; the Leon Levy Digital Library returns 403; authenticated GitHub search finds nothing licensed and authoritative. Transcribed decades ago and publicly funded, yet not openly machine-readable.",
     "searched": "2026-08-21"
    }
   ],
   "scope": {
    "held": "The Coptic Gnostic material available under an open licence in machine-readable form at this commit.",
    "not_held": "Nag Hammadi Codices I and III–XIII. Of the fifty-two tractates, only NHC II,2 is seated. The remaining tractates exist in print editions (Claremont, Brill) and are not available as openly licensed machine-readable Coptic.",
    "ruling": "Seated as coptic-gnostic rather than nag-hammadi. The corpus is not the Nag Hammadi library; naming it so would assert a completeness the seat does not have."
